{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788190926,"nonce":"AuepgGdzbUReYiI6UJvsOyhRgdk=","message":"CORRECTION TO THIS ISSUE'S DIAGNOSIS — I got the cause wrong, and a fix aimed at\nwhat I wrote would have left the real instability in place.\n\nI filed this as \"spawning a pawn reordered the existing entries\". **That is not\nwhat happened.** `pawns` was ALREADY deterministically sorted — attention\ndescending with an id tie-break — so inserting one pawn cannot permute the\nothers. What actually moves them is that `Attention` sums `100 - mood_pct`: two\ncolonists one mood point apart trade places on any tick that moves either\nmood, spawn or no spawn. The instability I observed was real; my attribution of\nit to the spawn was a coincidence of timing, and I stated it as fact.\n\n`thingIDNumber` comes from `Find.UniqueIDsManager.GetNextThingID()`, a scribed\nmonotonic counter, so a fresh pawn does always sort last — which is why the\n\"spawning appends\" story looked right.\n\nTHE OTHER THING I GOT WRONG: this issue framed the two options as either/or\n(\"these are not exclusive\" was hedged, but the acceptance implied one). **Both\nwere needed.** A stable index is still the wrong way to pick a pawn that must\nhave a CAPABILITY, which is what actually cost 3.4 six checks — the actor\ncouldn't haul. So the worker did both: `pawns` emits by id, and 3.4's phase 0\nnow selects the actor by predicate (first colonist with neither `Hauling` nor\n`Doctor` disabled) rather than by position. That also retires the\n\"make EVERY colonist suitable\" over-constraint I had put on the bench fixture.\n\nTHE SHAPE OF THE FIX, which is better than what I asked for: selection and\npresentation are now separate concerns. Attention-desc still decides WHICH\nentries survive the cap — 2.6's rule that a cap must never hide the downed\ncolonist is preserved — and the re-sort to id order runs on the survivors only.\nThe result publishes both facts (`order`, `selected_by`) and every line carries\n`attention_rank`, so urgency survives the reorder instead of being destroyed by\nit.\n\nNEW WRITE-ON-READ FOUND EN ROUTE, previously uncatalogued and now in DESIGN.md:\n`Pawn_PlayerSettings.displayOrder` — the field the colonist bar and the\nAssign/Work tables actually draw by, via `PlayerPawnsDisplayOrderUtility` —\ndefaults to sentinel `-9999999` and is assigned LAZILY BY THE UI, with\n`ColonistBar.CheckRecacheEntries` writing that scribed field. That is Class A.\nIt is also the obvious thing a future implementer would reach for to get \"the\norder the player sees\", so catching it before anyone did is worth more than the\nsort itself.\n\nBranch `spec/stable-pawn-order`, 4 commits, rebased on main, clean `-c Release`\nbuild (0 warnings). Merge preview against main: no conflicts. NOT YET VERIFIED\nIN GAME — `accept/1eb2262-stable-pawn-order.md` has the envelopes, including a\n\"what this does not prove\" section. Stays open until the orchestrator runs them.","files":null}]}